00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.570
YUFENG GUO: Did you know that
you can convert a Keras model

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.000
to a TensorFlow estimator?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.240
It'll give you a whole host
of options around distributed

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.430
training and scaling.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.120
Stay tuned to find out how.

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.570
Welcome to "Cloud
AI Adventures,"

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.860
where we explore
the art, science,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.140
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.360
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.430
And on this episode,
we are going

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:35.630
to prepare a Keras model for
running at scale by converting

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:40.070
it to a TensorFlow estimator.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.870
So we have a Keras model--

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.400
easy to define, clear to read,
and friendly to maintain.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.960
But it doesn't do so good with
scaling up to larger datasets

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.150
or running across many machines.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.460
Luckily, Keras and
TensorFlow have

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.510
some fantastic
interoperability features.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.990
What we want to do is
convert our Keras model

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:07.340
to a TensorFlow estimator,
which comes with the distributed

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.560
training built in.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.090
That's our ticket to solving
our scaling challenges.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.970
Plus it makes it easy
to do model serving

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.980
once our training is complete.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:20.900
The function that
we are interested in

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.247
is called model_to_estimator.

00:01:23.247 --> 00:01:24.830
And I've taken the
notebook we started

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:28.940
with in the previous episode
and updated it with new code

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.420
to convert our Keras model
to a TensorFlow estimator.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.970
We'll also take a look at
how to export your Keras

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.060
model as is once training
is complete, along

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.430
with the TensorFlow model.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:43.280
Let's switch over now to
the Kaggle notebook and dive

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.810
into the code.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.470
So here we are back
in our Kaggle kernel

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.260
with our Fashion MNIST dataset.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.630
This code should look
eminently familiar.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.870
We're going to load up
NumPy, pandas, TensorFlow,

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.350
and that plotlib.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.460
And we're going to
pull in our dataset

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.110
with pandas, reading
those CSV files,

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.540
and take a peek at the
first couple of rows.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.039
We can see that the first
column is our label column

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:11.420
and all our pixels
are all the rest

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.680
of the columns to the right.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.440
I've got this handy
preprocessing function,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.850
which takes our data
frame and pulls out

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.910
the values for the
features and the labels.

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.550
And then we can
save them directly

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:29.810
into the variables, like
train features, train labels,

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.650
test features, and test labels.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.130
Then we'll go ahead and
do our one-hot encoding.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:39.840
This is exactly the same as we
saw in our previous episode.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.920
We're going to convert those
labels, which were numbers,

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.510
to labels that are arrays
of length 10 for our 10

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.535
different possible labels.

00:02:50.535 --> 00:02:52.220
Then we'll run our train.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.780
We've got our
training parameters

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.250
and we'll create
our Keras model.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.210
This is exactly the same code
that we saw to create our Keras

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:00.680
model before.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.880
And once we have that created,
we can run model.fit--

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.050
identical to what we saw
in the previous episode.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.760
Once we have our model fit,
we can go ahead and test it.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.220
And now, we get to the
interesting stuff--

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.350
our conversion.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.160
How do we convert our Keras
model to TensorFlow estimator?

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.710
We'll use model_to_estimator.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.966
This function takes a couple
of different arguments.

00:03:23.966 --> 00:03:25.340
But in our case,
we're just going

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.090
to pass it the one
pointer to our model--

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.990
to the argument Keras model.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.660
And that will convert
our Keras model

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.770
into a TensorFlow estimator.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.740
Once we have our TensorFlow
estimator in hand,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.330
we can do everything
we would normally

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.460
do with a TensorFlow estimator.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.730
In this case, I've chosen
to go ahead and train it.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.130
We should train our
estimator and see

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:52.410
what we can achieve in terms
of accuracy and performance.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.550
So if we want to
do that, first we

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.510
need to think about how do we
architect our input function.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.720
And the big question
there is for our features,

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.090
we need a label
for those features.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.271
What do we call it?

00:04:07.271 --> 00:04:09.770
When we're coming from Keras,
we don't really get to name it

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:10.560
ourselves.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.190
We have to run
model.input_names.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:17.570
And this is the input names
of the Keras model, not

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:19.190
the TensorFlow model.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.149
Those names are copied over.

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:24.110
And so we can look
at our Keras model

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.230
and see that, in this case,
it's called dense_3_input.

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:30.860
And when we pull it out,
we'll use that variable input

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:35.330
name, which points
to dense_3_input,

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:40.790
as our key for our features.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:42.800
And so once we have
that set up, we

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.550
can do pretty much
the same as what

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:51.050
we're used to seeing with
TensorFlow estimator training.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.690
We'll have our feature columns.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:56.382
In this case, I'm going to
rename our tf classifier

00:04:56.382 --> 00:04:58.400
as just classifier.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:04:59.875
And now, we can train.

00:04:59.875 --> 00:05:02.390
And since we used the
correct input names,

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:04.486
we can do this
without any errors.

00:05:04.486 --> 00:05:06.110
If you don't use the
right input names,

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:09.860
you will get errors, so be sure
to get that input name out.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:12.020
Now, we can run
evaluate and set up

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.680
that evaluate_input function.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.070
You'll notice that we'll need
to use that input name once

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:19.070
again here, so a really
good idea to save that off

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:20.380
to a variable.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.830
Another thing to notice here is
that we used the one-hot labels

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.270
in the TensorFlow
training and evaluation,

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:30.620
just like we did in Keras.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.600
Now when we ran this
model in pure TensorFlow

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.150
in the past with
Fashion MNIST, we

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:41.350
were able to use the
not-one-hot encoded labels.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:44.950
We could just use the labels
that were 5, and 7, and 9.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:49.000
In this case, because we took
the model directly from Keras

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.900
and moved it to
TensorFlow, that conversion

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.780
also resulted in
the model expecting

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.290
one-hot encoded labels.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.790
And so you'll need to make
sure you use the same labels

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.620
as you did in Keras.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:06.250
In practice, this ends up being
really convenient because you

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.860
can just use the same
variables in the same arrays

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.080
that you used before.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.590
Now, we can see that the
evaluation is completed.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:18.220
In this case, we got to 86%.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:22.336
And in Keras, I think we
got something similar.

00:06:22.336 --> 00:06:24.820
We can see here-- yeah, 85%.

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:26.590
The accuracies
will vary depending

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.870
on a number of different
factors ranging

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:32.080
from the random
initialization state, how

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.500
my computer is feeling on a
given day, things like that.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.370
Now that we have our models
trained in both Keras

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.200
and TensorFlow, let's
take a brief look

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.560
at how we would export
these models out

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.420
into the file system.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.270
Keras models are very
straightforward to export.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:52.320
You just call model.save
and give it a name,

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.490
and it will export it
in the HDF5 format.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.610
On the TensorFlow side,
exporting our model

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:05.180
requires the use of the
export_savedmodel function.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:07.490
This function takes
two arguments.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.830
One is a path to export it to.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:14.420
And secondly, a serving
input receiver function,

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:18.680
which for short is often just
called a serving function.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.250
TensorFlow has a
utility to help you

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.110
build that serving function.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:25.720
And that takes, as an
argument, this mapping

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.630
of the input shape and type.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:32.570
This helps the model know
what kind of inputs to expect.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:35.830
So once we run our
export_savedmodel

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.550
on our TensorFlow model, we can
see where it was exported to.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:43.420
And we can see that
TensorFlow exports models

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.880
as a file and a folder
called variables.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:50.620
So for convenience, I also
like to zip it up or tar it

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:52.179
up so that it's
easy to download.

00:07:52.179 --> 00:07:54.220
And then on the other
side, once you download it,

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:58.240
you can unzip it
with tar as well.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.670
So that's kind of
what's involved

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.770
with taking a Keras model.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.260
We've taken that, converted
it to a TensorFlow estimator,

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.550
and then exported that
TensorFlow estimator.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:09.550
We've managed to do all
of this without actually

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.480
creating a TensorFlow
estimator from scratch.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:16.150
We've gone all the way
from taking a Keras model,

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:19.270
training it, converting it
to a TensorFlow estimator,

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:22.010
training that, and then
exporting the two of them.

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.490
And we'll be able to
use those exported files

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:29.080
in future episodes to do
more interesting things.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:32.890
Getting distributed training
using TensorFlow estimators

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:36.919
is an easy one-line adjustment
to your Keras model.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:39.970
So now, you have the
best of both worlds--

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:43.450
easy to read Keras model
creation syntax along

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:49.090
with distributed training
via TensorFlow estimators.

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:52.300
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:54.760
And if you enjoyed
it, please like it

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.360
and subscribe to get all
the latest episodes right

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.880
when they come out.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.710
For now, remember
that all you need

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:06.250
to get distributed training
working with your Keras model

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:10.140
is to use model_to_estimator.

